Le mardi 3 octobre 2017 01:03:13 UTC, stevo a =C3=A9crit :
>
> pi re loi mu gerku :
> Quantity 0.2 of a part of one of groups that consists of dogs that are fi=
ve
> (It is one dog.)
>
> Why does this not also mean an arbitrary group of parts of the group of=
=20
> five dogs, such as all their heads or legs?=20
>
> stevo
>

=20
Depending on the interpretation of combination of {gerku} and {si'e}, it=20
can mean heads or tails of dogs, but I don't think that interpretation=20
reflects a usual meaning of {gerku} and {si'e}.=20

{pi PA SUMTI} is defined as {lo pi PA si'e be pa me SUMTI}. According to=20
usual meaning of {si'e}, the referant of x1 of {pi PA si'e} should have the=
=20
same property as the referent of x2 of {pi PA si'e}, though that=20
interpretation is not officially declared. As shown in the example {pi re=
=20
lo mu gerku}, some selbrisni like {gerku} do not fit the usual meaning of=
=20
{si'e} because a part of dog is not a dog in usual meaning.=20

In the case of {pi re loi mu gerku}, which is developed as {lo pi re si'e=
=20
be pa me lo gunma be lo mu gerku}, x2 of {pi re si'e} refers to a=20
collective group of the referent of {gerku} in the meaning of xorlo, and=20
then the referent of x2 has the property of {gerku}. Therefore, according=
=20
to the usual meaning of {si'e}, x1 of {pi re si'e} should have the property=
=20
of {gerku}. However, according to the usual meaning of {gerku}, a head or a=
=20
tail of {gerku} does not have the property of {gerku}. In order to satisfy=
=20
the usual meaning of both, x1 of {pi re si'e} should be one dog.

mu'o

